  "slug": "blastomere-central-spindle-6ae1",
  "summary": "New C. elegans, Drosophila, Xenopus and zebrafish studies link DREAM repression, translation control and phosphorylation to narrowing developmental competence.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how regulatory layers constrain developmental competence and cell-cycle programs. In C. elegans, blastomeres stay competent for muscle fate until late 16E, with LIN-54/DREAM and MET-2 restricting plasticity. DREAM also represses zen-4, cyk-4 and spd-1 at the intestinal transition to endomitosis, blocking cytokinesis. Post-transcriptional and post-translational control appear elsewhere: Bicaudal-C silences translation through CCR4-NOT, Lsm14, 4E-T and Ddx6 in Xenopus axis patterning; CDK1-MAPK sites are dephosphorylated by Calcineurin at Drosophila egg activation; and -1 frameshifting generates a neural stem cell Zelda proteoform with tissue-specific pioneer activity. New here is DREAM acting on both fate and cytokinetic gene programs. Zebrafish M rRNA transcription is excluded as a female sex determinant, and rRNA biogenesis disruption causes histone mRNA misprocessing in C. elegans.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
